LANDMARK PRINCIPLE: Foundational Civil Liberties and Doctrine of Reasonable Restrictions.

CONSTITUTIONAL PROVISION:
19(1) All citizens shall have the right:
(a) to freedom of speech and expression;
(b) to assemble peaceably and without arms;
(c) to form associations or unions or co-operative societies;
(d) to move freely throughout the territory of India;
(e) to reside and settle in any part of the territory of India;
(g) to practise any profession, or to carry on any occupation, trade or business.

REASONABLE RESTRICTIONS:
19(2) Speech restrictions: sovereignty and integrity of India, security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, contempt of court, defamation, or incitement to an offence.
19(3)-(6) Restrictions on other freedoms in interests of sovereignty, integrity, public order, and general public.

LEGAL ANALYSIS:
Article 19 guarantees core democratic freedoms exclusively to Indian citizens. Restrictions imposed under clauses (2) to (6) must satisfy three mandatory constitutional tests: (1) Must be enacted by authority of law (not mere executive fiat); (2) Must fall strictly within the enumerated grounds of restrictions; and (3) Must be 'reasonable', adhering to the four-pronged test of proportionality (Modern Dental College, Anuradha Bhasin). The Supreme Court has expanded Article 19(1)(a) to encompass freedom of the press (Romesh Thappar, Bennett Coleman), right to commercial speech (Tata Press), right to information and voters' right to know (ADR), and freedom of speech over the internet (Shreya Singhal, Anuradha Bhasin).

LANDMARK PRINCIPLE: Moral and Civic Duties of Indian Citizens to the Republic.

CONSTITUTIONAL PROVISION:
It shall be the duty of every citizen of India:
(a) to abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem;
(b) to cherish and follow the noble ideals of the national struggle for freedom;
(c) to uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India;
(d) to defend the country and render national service when called upon;
(e) to promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional diversities; to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women;
(f) to value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture;
(g) to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures;
(h) to develop scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform;
(i) to safeguard public property and to abjure violence;
(j) to strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity;
(k) who is a parent or guardian to provide opportunities for education to his child between six and fourteen years (added by 86th Amendment, 2002).

LEGAL ANALYSIS:
Inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976) based on the Swaran Singh Committee report, Fundamental Duties are non-justiciable per se, but serve as vital interpretative tools for courts. In AIIMS Students' Union v. AIIMS (2002), the Supreme Court ruled that fundamental duties cannot be overlooked and must be read alongside fundamental rights to assess the reasonableness of legislative restrictions under Articles 19 and 14.
